## 6. Findings

Severity scale: HIGH = would change a decision; MED = add an obligation or change a citation's strength; LOW = wording precision. None are HIGH.

- **C1 [LOW, precision] — PR #533 undo-entry timing.** The proposal says the entry is created because `_block_saving` "is set at mouse-press and cleared at mouse-release so one paint stroke = one undo entry." The patch shows the entry is saved **at mouse-press before the stroke mutates data** (`self._save_history()` then `self._block_saving = True` in `on_mouse_press`), and `_block_saving` suppresses the `_save_history()` calls inside subsequent `paint()`/`fill()` calls until `on_mouse_release` clears it. Also, history trimming pops the **oldest undo entry when the combined undo+redo length exceeds the limit**. Effect ("one stroke = one pre-stroke-state entry") is as the proposal describes; affected dependency: Section 3 mechanism description and the Section 5 undo design (no design change; if anything the press-time snapshot is the right model for our command log: record state before mutation).
- **C2 [LOW, precision] — axes MUST/SHOULD split.** The proposal summarizes "axes carry unique `name`, `type`, and `unit`" without the modality split: name uniqueness is MUST; `type` and `unit` are SHOULD (and `type` MAY be a custom string). Affected dependency: Section 6 import validator must **tolerate** NGFF stores with missing `type`/`unit` (or custom types), surfacing them as an explicit user-confirmed assumption (pixels/dimensionless) exactly as already planned for PNG/JPEG, rather than hard-requiring the fields.
- **C3 [LOW, precision] — labels constraints are informative, not normative.** "Only integer values are supported" and "dims match image or 1" live in the §2.1 layout-example **comments**, not in a normative MUST clause of NGFF 0.4. The proposal's citation [FACT S2 §2.1] is factually placed but should not imply normative force. Affected dependency: Section 4.2 — our own project contract should state integer-only masks and dims-matching as **our** normative rules (NGFF 0.4 will not enforce them for us).
- **C4 [MED, addition] — multiscales transform obligations strengthen the import validator.** The captured §3.4 adds rules the proposal does not list but that its affine design matches exactly: dataset coordinateTransformations "MUST only be of type `translation` or `scale`"; "MUST contain exactly one `scale`"; translation, if present, "MUST be listed after `scale`"; scale/translation lengths MUST equal the axes length; datasets paths ordered largest→smallest. Also `path`-referenced binary transforms exist in the vocabulary (the proposal only describes inline vectors; our exporter uses inline vectors — fine, but the importer should either support or visibly reject `path` forms). Affected dependency: Section 6 support boundary — adopt these as concrete import-validation rules and rejection messages.
- **C5 [LOW, catalog precision] — the pinned page is revision 0.4.1.** The captured page's Version History shows the latest revision as **0.4.1, 2022-09-26** ("transitional metadata for image collections ('bioformats2raw.layout')"), with 0.4.0 dated 2022-02-08. The proposal/catalog cite "OME-NGFF 0.4 … updated 2026-10-01" (presumably a page-delivery date). Affected dependency: pin wording in any final document should read "NGFF 0.4 line, page revision 0.4.1 (2022-09-09–26 content)" — no design change; the normative sections the proposal relies on (§3.1–3.4) are unaffected by the 0.4.1 transitional-metadata addition, though an importer **may encounter** `bioformats2raw.layout` collections in the wild (§3.2 of the same page) and should handle multi-image groups explicitly rather than silently opening only the first image (the spec itself advises readers to make users aware of multiple images — line 2600).
- **C6 [honest gap] — one sub-claim not re-verified in this pass.** The literal sentence "This is the 0.4 release of this specification. Migration scripts will be provided between numbered versions" (and the editor's-draft caveat) is recorded in catalog S2 with a byte-range locator, but my probes of the linemap did not reach the page-header region containing it. I did verify adjacent structure (§6 Citing "This edition … /0.4/", §7 Version History). Resolution for the next candidate: read the linemap in the approximate byte range 126000–130000 (immediately before the TOC tail seen at 130000) and quote the sentence; until then, treat A3's "migration scripts promise" as **catalog-backed, not independently re-verified**. The 0.4-pin decision itself does not hinge on this sentence.

